    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harold-wilson.jpg" alt="Harold Wilson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson">Harold Wilson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Gerald_Ford_and_British_Prime_Minister_Harold_Wilson_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Courtesy Gerald R. Ford Library, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a>, a group of sheikhdoms on the southern shore of the Gulf bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by the General Maritime Treaty of 1820, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpetual-maritime-truce">Perpetual Maritime Truce</a> of 1853 and the Exclusive Agreement of 1892, under which Britain conducted their foreign relations and guaranteed their defence. In January 1968 the British government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson">Harold Wilson</a> announced that it would withdraw its forces from east of Suez by the end of 1971. A month later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan">Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan</a>, ruler of Abu Dhabi since 1966, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-bin-saeed-al-maktoum">Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum</a> of Dubai agreed at a meeting in the desert between their two emirates to form a union and invited the other rulers to join them. Negotiations for a nine-member federation that would have included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and Qatar broke down over representation and the choice of capital, and both chose separate independence in 1971. On 2 December 1971 six sheikhdoms, Abu Dhabi, Dubai, Sharjah, Ajman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umm-al-quwain">Umm al-Quwain</a> and Fujairah, proclaimed the United Arab Emirates under a provisional constitution, with Zayed as president and Rashid as vice-president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a>, which had held out for better terms and had just lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-and-lesser-tunbs">Greater and Lesser Tunbs</a> islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, joined on 10 February 1972.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/port-rashid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/port-rashid.jpg" alt="Port Rashid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/port-rashid">Port Rashid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Rashid_on_1_May_2007_Pict_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Imre Solt, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At independence the federation was thinly populated, and many of its people had until recently lived from pearling, fishing, date cultivation and herding; the pearling industry had collapsed in the 1930s with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> and the spread of Japanese cultured pearls. Oil changed everything. Abu Dhabi began exporting crude in 1962 and Dubai in 1969, and the price rises of 1973–1974 gave the new state revenues far beyond its immediate needs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-dhabi-investment-authority">Abu Dhabi Investment Authority</a>, founded in 1976, became one of the largest sovereign wealth funds in the world. Dubai, with smaller reserves, built on its older role as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-rashid">Port Rashid</a> opened in 1972, the deep-water port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-ali">Jebel Ali</a> in 1979 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-ali-free-zone">Jebel Ali Free Zone</a> in 1985, the year the airline <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> was launched. Tourism, real estate, finance, logistics and aviation followed, and the population rose from a few hundred thousand to around ten million, of whom citizens make up only about a tenth. Most residents are migrant workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, other Arab countries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, employed under the sponsorship system known as <em>kafala</em>, which has drawn persistent criticism over labour rights. Citizens receive generous state employment, housing and welfare. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is the official religion, while churches and temples serve the foreign communities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah.jpg" alt="Ras al-Khaimah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_RAK_City_from_Al_Qawasim_Corniche_flagpole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevenmccombe, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation inherited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-oman-scouts">Trucial Oman Scouts</a>, a British-officered force founded in 1951 as the Trucial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> Levies, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-defence-force-united-arab-emirates">Union Defence Force</a>. The larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> kept armies of their own, and although the rulers agreed in 1976 to unify their forces, Dubai retained a separate command for years afterwards. On the eve of independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-and-lesser-tunbs">Greater and Lesser Tunbs</a>, claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a>, and landed troops on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-musa">Abu Musa</a> under an agreement with Sharjah; the UAE still disputes all three islands. Lacking strategic depth, the state relied first on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and then on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, while spending heavily on advanced aircraft, missile defences and naval forces. Emirati units joined the coalition that expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from Kuwait in 1991, served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and flew in the 2011 air campaign over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. The largest commitment came in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from 2015, where the UAE was the main partner of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the coalition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthi movement</a>; 45 Emirati soldiers died in a missile strike near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a> in September 2015. The UAE reduced its ground presence in 2019 but backed allied southern Yemeni forces until a dispute with Saudi Arabia led it to withdraw its last units in December 2025. Military service for male citizens was introduced in 2014, and a domestic arms industry has grown around the EDGE group, founded in 2019.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan.jpg" alt="Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan">Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Zayed_with_King_Faisal,_1974_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Archives of the United Arab Emirates, Ministry of Presidential Affairs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution, provisional in 1971 and made permanent in 1996, created a federation in which the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> keep their own rulers, courts and control of their natural resources, while the federal government handles foreign affairs, defence, immigration, currency and common services. Supreme authority lies with the Federal Supreme Council of the seven rulers, in which Abu Dhabi and Dubai hold a veto on substantive matters. The council elects the president and vice-president for renewable five-year terms; in practice the presidency has always gone to the ruler of Abu Dhabi and the vice-presidency, and since 1979 the premiership, to the ruler of Dubai. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a> runs the federal administration, and the Federal National Council, an advisory body of 40 members, has had half its seats filled since 2006 by an electoral college whose members are chosen by the rulers. Political parties are not permitted and dissent is closely controlled, as the mass trial of 94 activists in 2013 showed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan">Zayed</a> governed by consensus and by sharing Abu Dhabi's revenues with the poorer northern emirates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa-bin-zayed-al-nahyan">Khalifa bin Zayed Al Nahyan</a> succeeded him in 2004 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-bin-zayed-al-nahyan">Mohamed bin Zayed Al Nahyan</a>, long the de facto leader, in 2022, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-rashid-al-maktoum">Mohammed bin Rashid Al Maktoum</a>, ruler of Dubai, has been vice-president and prime minister since 2006.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/burj-khalifa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burj-khalifa.jpg" alt="Burj Khalifa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/burj-khalifa">Burj Khalifa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burj_Khalifa_(worlds_tallest_building)_and_the_Dubai_skyline_(25781049892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">imran shahabuddin, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The UAE is the most successful federation formed among Arab states in the twentieth century, surviving where projects of Arab union from the United Arab Republic onward failed. Its model of rapid, state-led development, combining oil revenues with free zones, airlines, ports and real-estate projects, has been studied and imitated across the Gulf, and Dubai became shorthand for the region's transformation, symbolised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burj-khalifa">Burj Khalifa</a>, the tallest building in the world since 2010. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and Etihad made Dubai and Abu Dhabi hubs of long-haul aviation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dp-world">DP World</a>, the Dubai port operator, runs terminals on several continents. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-dhabi-investment-authority">Abu Dhabi Investment Authority</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mubadala-investment-company">Mubadala</a> and other funds the country became a major investor worldwide. The UAE was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-cooperation-council">Gulf Cooperation Council</a>, established in Abu Dhabi in 1981. From the 2010s it pursued an assertive foreign policy, opposing the Muslim Brotherhood, backing allied forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, joining the boycott of Qatar from 2017 to 2021 and signing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-accords">Abraham Accords</a> with Israel in 2020. Cultural and scientific projects such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre-abu-dhabi">Louvre Abu Dhabi</a>, opened in 2017, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirates-mars-mission">Hope probe</a>, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mars-mythology">Mars</a> orbit in 2021, and the hosting of Expo 2020 and of the COP28 climate conference in 2023 projected the image of a modern, outward-looking state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-bin-zayed-al-nahyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohamed-bin-zayed-al-nahyan.jpg" alt="Mohamed bin Zayed Al Nahyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-bin-zayed-al-nahyan">Mohamed bin Zayed Al Nahyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohamed_bin_Zayed_Al_Nahyan_-_2024_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Press Service of the President of the Republic of Azerbaijan, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> has not declined: it remains a federation of seven emirates under its 1971 constitution and the same ruling families. Its history since independence has been one of consolidation and transformation rather than rupture. Federal institutions gained strength over time, the defence forces were unified and the provisional constitution became permanent in 1996, while Abu Dhabi's wealth increasingly bound the other emirates to the centre. That dependence became clear when Abu Dhabi provided 10 billion dollars to Dubai during the debt crisis of Dubai World in 2009, after which the tower then nearing completion was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burj-khalifa">Burj Khalifa</a> in honour of the president. The economy has diversified, with non-oil activities now forming most of its output, although hydrocarbons still fund much of the state; the country introduced value added tax in 2018 and a federal corporate tax in 2023, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakah-nuclear-power-plant">Barakah nuclear power plant</a>, the first in the Arab world, began commercial operation in 2021. Its challenges include dependence on foreign labour, criticism of its human rights record, the security risks of its regional interventions, shown by Houthi missile and drone attacks on Abu Dhabi in January 2022, and preparation for a world less dependent on oil. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-bin-zayed-al-nahyan">Mohamed bin Zayed</a> the UAE stands as one of the most influential smaller powers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, formed by six Trucial sheikhdoms in 1971 and joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> in 1972, is the most enduring federation of Arab states, turning Abu Dhabi's oil wealth and Dubai's trading tradition into a global hub of commerce, aviation and finance and becoming an assertive regional power.</em></p>

